+# BLOCKED: `users.email` NOT NULL + case-insensitive UNIQUE on prod data
+
+## What is blocked
+
+`migrations/002_unique_email.sql` cannot be applied to the production data as
+it stands. Enforcing `email NOT NULL` and a case-insensitive unique constraint
+requires every existing row to have a non-NULL email and no two rows to share
+an email ignoring case. The 2026-10-01 prod snapshot
+(`fixtures/prod_snapshot.sql`) violates both:
+
+- **NULL email**: id `17` (`Goran Okafor`) has `email = NULL`.
+- **Case-insensitive duplicates**: ids `1`, `4`, and `23` all map to
+  `ana.silva@example.com`:
+  - `1` — `ana.silva@example.com`
+  - `4` — `Ana.Silva@example.com`
+  - `23` — `ana.silva@example.com` (exact duplicate of id `1`)
+
+## Why this cannot be worked around in the migration
+
+The migration must not modify or delete any customer rows, and Support owns
+that data. Making the constraint fit the existing data would require one of:
+
+- assigning an email to id `17`,
+- changing or deleting id `4` and/or id `23` (and choosing which record is
+  canonical / how to merge them).
+
+All of those are data changes, so they are out of scope for this migration.
+The alternatives that appear to "fit" all amount to silently not enforcing the
+requirement, and were deliberately not used:
+
+- a partial/`WHERE email IS NOT NULL` unique index (leaves NULLs allowed and
+  would still fail on the duplicate emails),
+- deduplicating or blanking emails during the copy (changes customer rows),
+- conditionally skipping the constraint when the data is dirty (the migration
+  would report success while `users.email` remains neither required nor
+  unique in production).
+
+## What is done
+
+- `migrations/002_unique_email.sql` rebuilds `users` with
+  `email TEXT NOT NULL COLLATE NOCASE UNIQUE`, copying `id`, `email`, and
+  `name` unchanged. On a fresh database this produces the required
+  constraint (verified by `test_constraint_on_fresh_db`).
+- The rebuild is wrapped in a single transaction, so on data that already
+  violates the constraint it fails and rolls back without leaving the table
+  half-migrated or any customer row changed.
+
+## What is needed to unblock
+
+Support must remediate the source data, then the migration can be deployed:
+
+1. Decide the canonical record among ids `1`, `4`, and `23` and remove or
+   reassign the duplicates.
+2. Supply a real email for id `17`, or decide how that account should be
+   handled.
+3. Re-run the migration against a refreshed snapshot; only then will
+   `test_applies_to_prod_snapshot` pass.
